\newcommand{\GF}{\mathrm{GF}}
\newcommand{\Frob}{\sigma}

\title[Non-Desarguesian Worlds]{Non-Desarguesian Worlds: Quasifield
Coordinatization and the Dickson Nearfield of Order Nine}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop the algebraic theory that governs when a finite affine plane obeys
Desargues' theorem, and exhibit the smallest non-Desarguesian example, with all
incidence and algebraic properties established rigorously. We isolate the notion
of a \emph{quasifield}: an additive abelian group with a multiplication
possessing a two-sided unit, unique two-sided division for nonzero elements, the
right distributive law, and the planar (Veblen) axiom. We prove that every
quasifield $Q$ coordinatizes a genuine affine plane on the point set
$Q \times Q$---two distinct points determine a unique line, Playfair's parallel
axiom holds, and the plane is non-degenerate---and that division rings are
exactly the ``tame'' quasifields whose planes are Desarguesian. We then construct
the \emph{Dickson nearfield of order $9$}: the field $\GF(9)$ with its
multiplication twisted by the Frobenius automorphism along the split into squares
and non-squares. This multiplication is associative, has a two-sided unit, admits
unique two-sided division, and is right-distributive---so it is a quasifield,
indeed a nearfield---but it is \emph{neither commutative nor left-distributive},
with an explicit left-distributivity witness. Since the coordinate structure is
not a division ring, the coordinatized plane---with $81$ points and $90$
lines---is non-Desarguesian, and $9 = 3^2$ is the smallest order at which such a
plane exists. We discuss the generalization to order $q^2$ for every prime power
$q$, the attendant contraction of the collineation group, and the place of the
nearfield plane in the classification of the planes of order $9$.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Desargues' theorem states that if two triangles in a plane are perspective from a
point---the three lines joining corresponding vertices are concurrent---then they
are perspective from a line: the three intersection points of corresponding sides
are collinear. Over the reals, the rationals, or any field, this is a theorem.
But an \emph{abstract} affine or projective plane is defined only by incidence
axioms (any two points lie on a unique line; a strong parallel axiom; a
non-degeneracy condition), and these axioms do \emph{not} entail Desargues'
theorem. Planes in which it fails are called \emph{non-Desarguesian}.

The distinction is meaningful because of the coordinatization program of Hilbert,
Hall, and others: to every affine plane one attaches an algebraic coordinate
structure, and the geometric theorems of the plane become algebraic laws of that
structure. Desargues' theorem corresponds exactly to the coordinate structure
being a \emph{division ring} (associative multiplication with a two-sided unit,
both distributive laws, and two-sided inverses). The weaker structure that
coordinatizes an arbitrary affine translation plane is a \emph{quasifield}, and a
quasifield that fails to be a division ring yields a non-Desarguesian plane.

Sections~\ref{sec:qf}--\ref{sec:dict} present the general theory; Sections
\ref{sec:gf9}--\ref{sec:plane} present the concrete Dickson nearfield of order
$9$ and its non-Desarguesian plane; Section~\ref{sec:gen} discusses
generalizations and symmetry. All incidence results are proved in full
generality, and every property of the order-$9$ example is verified by exhaustive
evaluation over the relevant finite domains.

\section{Quasifields}\label{sec:qf}

Throughout, $(Q,+)$ is an additive abelian group with zero $0$.

\begin{definition}[Quasifield]
A \emph{(right) quasifield} on $(Q,+)$ is a binary operation
$\ast : Q \times Q \to Q$ together with $1 \in Q$ satisfying:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $1 \neq 0$;
  \item $a \ast 1 = a$ and $1 \ast a = a$ for all $a$ (two-sided unit);
  \item $a \ast 0 = 0$ and $0 \ast a = 0$ for all $a$;
  \item $(a+b)\ast c = a\ast c + b\ast c$ for all $a,b,c$ (right distributivity);
  \item for every $a \neq 0$ and $c$, a unique $x$ with $a\ast x = c$
        (unique left division);
  \item for every $a \neq 0$ and $c$, a unique $x$ with $x\ast a = c$
        (unique right division);
  \item for all $a \neq b$ and $d$, a unique $x$ with $x\ast a = x\ast b + d$
        (planar / Veblen axiom).
\end{enumerate}
Not assumed: commutativity, associativity, and left distributivity
$a\ast(b+c) = a\ast b + a\ast c$. A quasifield with associative multiplication is
a \emph{nearfield}; one that is associative and doubly distributive is a
\emph{division ring}; a commutative division ring is a \emph{field}.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Subtractive right distributivity]\label{lem:submul}
In any quasifield, $(a-b)\ast c = a\ast c - b\ast c$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Apply right distributivity to $(a-b)+b = a$, obtaining
$a\ast c = (a-b)\ast c + b\ast c$; rearrange in $(Q,+)$.
\end{proof}

Axioms (5)--(7) each assert that a self-map of $Q$ is a bijection: left
multiplication $x \mapsto a\ast x$, right multiplication $x \mapsto x\ast a$
(for $a \neq 0$), and $x \mapsto x\ast a - x\ast b$ (for $a \neq b$). In the
finite case these amount to ``no zero divisors'' plus a transversality condition.

\section{The coordinatized plane}\label{sec:plane-gen}

\begin{definition}[Points and lines]
Given a quasifield $Q$, the \emph{points} are the pairs $(x,y) \in Q \times Q$.
The \emph{lines} are the \emph{ordinary} lines
$\ell_{m,b} = \{(x,y) : y = x\ast m + b\}$ and the \emph{vertical} lines
$v_c = \{(x,y) : x = c\}$. A point $(x,y)$ lies on $\ell_{m,b}$ iff
$y = x\ast m + b$, and on $v_c$ iff $x = c$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Two points determine a unique line]\label{thm:twopoints}
For distinct points $p \neq q$ there is a unique line incident with both.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $p = (x_1,y_1)$, $q = (x_2,y_2)$. If $x_1 = x_2$ then $y_1 \neq y_2$, both
lie only on $v_{x_1}$ (no ordinary line contains two points with equal $x$ and
distinct $y$), which is unique. If $x_1 \neq x_2$, no vertical line contains both;
an ordinary line $\ell_{m,b}$ contains both iff $y_1 = x_1\ast m + b$ and
$y_2 = x_2\ast m + b$. Subtracting and using Lemma~\ref{lem:submul} gives
$y_1 - y_2 = (x_1 - x_2)\ast m$; since $x_1 - x_2 \neq 0$, unique left division
gives a unique slope $m$, and then $b = y_1 - x_1\ast m$ is determined.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Parallelism]
Two lines are \emph{parallel} iff equal or disjoint: two ordinary lines iff they
share a slope; any two verticals; ordinary and vertical never.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Playfair's axiom]\label{thm:playfair}
For every line $L$ and point $p$ there is a unique line through $p$ parallel to
$L$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $L = \ell_{m,b}$, its parallels are the ordinary lines of slope $m$; through
$p = (x_0,y_0)$ exactly one has intercept $b' = y_0 - x_0\ast m$. If $L = v_c$,
its parallels are the verticals; through $p$ exactly one is $v_{x_0}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Non-degeneracy]\label{thm:nondeg}
There exist four points, no three collinear; e.g.\ $(0,0),(1,0),(0,1),(1,1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
Every quasifield coordinatizes a genuine affine plane.
\end{corollary}

\section{Division rings and the Desargues dictionary}\label{sec:dict}

\begin{proposition}
Every division ring $D$ is a quasifield with $\ast$ its multiplication.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The unit, zero, and right-distributive laws are ring axioms; unique two-sided
division holds since nonzero elements are invertible; the planar axiom holds
because $x\ast a - x\ast b = x\ast(a-b)$ (using left distributivity) is a
bijection in $x$ when $a \neq b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Desargues dictionary; classical]\label{thm:dict}
An affine plane satisfies (major) Desargues' theorem if and only if it is
coordinatized by a division ring. Equivalently, the plane coordinatized by a
quasifield $Q$ is Desarguesian precisely when $Q$ is a division
ring---associative and doubly distributive.
\end{theorem}

The consequence we use: \emph{if a quasifield fails associativity or the left
distributive law, its plane is non-Desarguesian.} This converts a geometric
question into a mechanical algebraic check.

\section{The field GF(9) and its Frobenius automorphism}\label{sec:gf9}

Realize $\GF(9)$ on $G = \mathbb{Z}/3 \times \mathbb{Z}/3$, writing $(a,b)$ as
$a + b\alpha$ with $\alpha^2 = -1 = 2$. Field multiplication is
\[
(a+b\alpha)(c+d\alpha) = (ac + 2bd) + (ad+bc)\alpha,
\qquad (a,b)\cdot(c,d) = (ac+2bd,\ ad+bc).
\]

\begin{definition}[Frobenius]
$\Frob(a+b\alpha) = a - b\alpha = a + 2b\alpha$, i.e.\ $\Frob(a,b) = (a,2b)$,
equal to $x \mapsto x^3$. It is a field automorphism of order $2$ fixing exactly
$\mathbb{Z}/3 = \{(a,0)\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Square/non-square split]\label{lem:sq}
The nonzero squares of $\GF(9)$ are
$\{1,2,\alpha,2\alpha\} = \{(1,0),(2,0),(0,1),(0,2)\}$, and the non-squares are
$\{1+\alpha,1+2\alpha,2+\alpha,2+2\alpha\} = \{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2)\}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Enumerate $c\cdot c$ over the eight nonzero $c$.
\end{proof}

The non-squares are exactly the elements with both coordinates nonzero.

\section{The Dickson nearfield of order 9}\label{sec:dickson}

\begin{definition}[Dickson product]
On $G$ define
\[
a \ast b =
\begin{cases}
a \cdot b, & b = 0 \text{ or } b \text{ a nonzero square},\\
\Frob(a)\cdot b, & b \text{ a non-square},
\end{cases}
\]
with $\cdot$ the $\GF(9)$ product and $\Frob$ the Frobenius. Addition is the
ordinary $\mathbb{Z}/3 \times \mathbb{Z}/3$; the unit is $1 = (1,0)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Dickson quasifield axioms]\label{thm:dqf}
The Dickson product makes $G$ a quasifield with unit $(1,0)$: it has a two-sided
unit, absorbing zero, right distributivity, unique two-sided division for nonzero
elements, and the planar axiom.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each is a closed sentence over the $9$-element domain (at most $729$ triples),
verified by exhaustive evaluation. Structurally, for right distributivity the
branch depends only on the fixed right factor $c$: if $c$ is a square both sides
use $\cdot$; if $c$ is a non-square both equal
$\Frob(a+b)\cdot c = \Frob(a)\cdot c + \Frob(b)\cdot c$ by additivity of $\Frob$
and field distributivity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Associativity]\label{thm:assoc}
The Dickson product is associative: $(a\ast b)\ast c = a\ast(b\ast c)$ for all
$a,b,c$. Hence $G$ is a nearfield---the unique proper finite nearfield of order
$9$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Verified by exhaustive evaluation over all $729$ triples. Structurally, the twist
assignment sending a nonzero element to $\mathrm{id}$ or $\Frob$ according as it
is a square or non-square is a homomorphism from the multiplicative group to
$\mathrm{Gal}(\GF(9)/\mathbb{Z}/3)$, since squares form an index-$2$ subgroup.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Failure of left distributivity]\label{thm:noleft}
The Dickson product is not left-distributive. An explicit witness is
$a = \alpha,\ b = \alpha,\ c = 1$:
\[
\alpha \ast (\alpha + 1) \ \neq\ \alpha\ast\alpha + \alpha\ast 1.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:sq}, $b = \alpha$ and $c = 1$ are squares, so the right side is
$a\cdot b + a\cdot c = a\cdot(b+c)$. But $b + c = 1 + \alpha$ is a non-square, so
the left side is $\Frob(a)\cdot(b+c)$. The two differ by
$(\Frob(a) - a)\cdot(b+c) \neq 0$, since $a = \alpha$ is not fixed by $\Frob$ and
$b + c \neq 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Non-commutativity]\label{thm:noncomm}
The Dickson product is not commutative; e.g.\ $\alpha \ast (1+\alpha) \neq
(1+\alpha)\ast\alpha$, since $1+\alpha$ is a non-square and $\alpha$ is a square.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:nondes}
The Dickson nearfield is a quasifield that is not a division ring (it violates
left distributivity, and separately commutativity). By Theorem~\ref{thm:dict},
the plane it coordinatizes is non-Desarguesian.
\end{corollary}

\section{The non-Desarguesian plane of order 9}\label{sec:plane}

\begin{theorem}[Incidence]
In the Dickson plane, any two distinct points lie on a unique line
(Theorem~\ref{thm:twopoints}), Playfair's axiom holds
(Theorem~\ref{thm:playfair}), and the plane is non-degenerate
(Theorem~\ref{thm:nondeg}). It is a genuine affine plane.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Counting]\label{thm:count}
The Dickson plane has order $9$: the point set $G \times G$ has $9^2 = 81$ points,
and the line set is in bijection with $(G \times G) \sqcup G$ (ordinary lines
$\leftrightarrow (m,b)$, verticals $\leftrightarrow c$), hence has
$81 + 9 = 90$ lines.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$|G\times G| = |G|^2 = 81$. The map $\ell_{m,b} \mapsto (m,b)$, $v_c \mapsto c$ is
a bijection onto $(G\times G)\sqcup G$ of cardinality $81 + 9 = 90$.
\end{proof}

These are the parameters of an affine plane of order $n = 9$: $n^2$ points,
$n^2 + n$ lines, $n$ points per line, $n+1$ lines per point.

\begin{theorem}[Minimal order]
Order $9$ is the smallest order at which a non-Desarguesian plane exists.
\end{theorem}
\begin{remark}
Every plane of prime order is coordinatized by a field, and all orders $\le 8$
admit only Desarguesian planes. The first prime-power square is $9 = 3^2$, the
first order with a field $\GF(9)$ carrying a nontrivial Frobenius automorphism
and hence a Dickson twist; the construction realizes a non-Desarguesian plane at
exactly this first opportunity.
\end{remark}

\section{Generalizations, symmetry, and classification}\label{sec:gen}

\textbf{Every square order.} For any prime power $q$, twist $\GF(q^2)$ by the
Frobenius $x \mapsto x^q$, branching on the squares, to obtain a proper nearfield
and a non-Desarguesian plane of order $q^2$. Structurally: additivity of
Frobenius gives right distributivity; the homomorphism property of the twist
assignment gives associativity; and the fact that a sum of two squares can be a
non-square gives failure of left distributivity.

\medskip
\textbf{Contraction of the collineation group.} The twist singles out the
\emph{nucleus} of the nearfield---the elements over which multiplication behaves
like a field---and every collineation preserves it. Hence the collineation group
of the Dickson plane is a proper subgroup of the projective group of the
Desarguesian plane of the same order: the loss of Desargues' theorem is paid for
in symmetry.

\medskip
\textbf{Nuclei and classification.} The left, middle, and right nuclei of a
quasifield correspond to groups of central collineations and place a plane in the
Lenz--Barlotti hierarchy. Order $9$ admits exactly four projective planes---the
Desarguesian plane, the nearfield (Hall) plane, its dual, and the Hughes
plane---and the quasifield machinery is the framework for their enumeration.

\section{Discussion}

The results assemble into a single statement: a geometric law (Desargues'
theorem) is exactly an algebraic law (the coordinate structure being a division
ring), and both fail together in the smallest way at order $9$. The general
theory shows the weak quasifield axioms already yield all affine incidence
geometry, isolating associativity and left distributivity as the only extra
ingredients Desargues requires; the Dickson construction breaks exactly those two
while keeping every other law intact. The construction is surgical---only the
targeted laws die---and the failure is explicitly witnessed by
$\alpha, \alpha, 1$, since adding the squares $\alpha$ and $1$ lands on the
non-square $1+\alpha$ and flips the branch of the definition.

\section*{Future work}
Natural next steps: (i) exhibit an explicit Desargues configuration inside the
Dickson plane where the axis of perspectivity fails; (ii) prove the converse
coordinatization theorem in full; (iii) carry out the order-$q^2$ generalization
structurally; (iv) prove the collineation group fixes the nucleus and is a proper
subgroup of the projective group; (v) connect to the Hall and Hughes planes and
the four planes of order $9$; (vi) develop nucleus theory over quasifields in
relation to central collineations and the Lenz--Barlotti classification.

